{
  "abstract": "Let (Ω, μ) be a probability space and a = (aₖ) a family of m square-integrable functions with positive definite Gram matrix I = ∫ a a* dμ. Then for every n ≥ m there are points x₁, …, xₙ ∈ Ω, not necessarily distinct, with (1 − √((m−1)/n))² I ≼ (1/n) ∑ᵢ a(xᵢ) a(xᵢ)* in the Loewner order, that is (1 − √((m−1)/n))² ∫ |f|² dμ ≤ (1/n) ∑ᵢ |f(xᵢ)|² for every f in the span of the family. For bounded measurable functions there are n points with κ ‖f‖_p ≤ m^{1/2−1/p} ((2/n) ∑ᵢ |f(xᵢ)|²)^{1/2} for every 2 ≤ p ≤ ∞ at once, where κ = max(1 − √(m/n), 1/(2m)). This is Proposition 8 of Chkifa, Dolbeault, Krieg and Ullrich, with n points in place of 2n. Up to constants such bounds are due to Bartel, Schäfer and Ullrich, and to Limonova and Temlyakov under a Nikol'skii-type inequality.",
